Equation of $P Q$, is
$\begin{aligned}
& \frac{x-1}{1} \\
\therefore \quad & =\frac{y+5}{1}=\frac{z-9}{1}=\lambda \\
& x & =\lambda+1\\
\text { and } 
y & =\lambda-5 \\
z & =\lambda+9
\end{aligned}$
lies on the plane $x-y+z=5\begin{array}{ll}
\Rightarrow & \lambda+1-\lambda+5+\lambda+9=5 \\
\Rightarrow & \lambda=-10
\end{array}
$\therefore$ Co-ordinates of $Q$ are $Q(-9,-15,-1)\Rightarrow \quad P Q=\sqrt{10^2+10^2+10^2}=10 \sqrt{3}

Let slope of a line be $m$.
Now, the equation of a line passing through $(1,0)$ is
$\begin{aligned}
y-0 & =m(x-1) \\
\Rightarrow m x-y-m & =0
\end{aligned}$<br/>Distance from origin $=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2}\begin{array}{ll}
\Rightarrow & \frac{|-m|}{\sqrt{1+m^2}}=\frac{\sqrt{3}}{2} \\
\Rightarrow & 4 m^2=3\left(1+m^2\right) \\
\Rightarrow & m^2=3 \\
\Rightarrow & m= \pm \sqrt{3}
\end{array}
$\therefore$ Equations of lines are
$\begin{array}{lrl} 
& \sqrt{3} x-y-\sqrt{3} & =0 \\
\text { and } & -\sqrt{3} x-y+\sqrt{3} & =0 \\
\Rightarrow  & \sqrt{3} x-y-\sqrt{3}=0 \\
\text { and } & \sqrt{3} x+y-\sqrt{3}=0
\end{array}

